  "answer_text": "Here's a comprehensive breakdown of how much mutual fund managers make (in terms of fees charged):\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcb0 Mutual Fund Manager Fees\n\n### \ud83d\udccc Overall Range\nManagement fees, whether paid as a mutual fund expense ratio or a fee paid to a financial advisor, **typically range from 0.01% to over 2%**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcca Types of Fees\n\n#### 1. **Operating Fees / Expense Ratios**\nOperating fees, also known as mutual fund expense ratios or advisory fees, are typically **between 0.25% and 1%** of your total investment per year.\n\nThese typically cover:\n- **Management fees** (to pay fund managers and investment advisors), **12b-1 fees** (capped at 1%, for marketing and shareholder services), and **other expenses** such as custodial, legal, accounting, and administrative costs.\n\n#### 2. **Wrap Fees**\nSometimes an investment manager will consolidate a client's various fees into a **wrap fee**, which may encompass the management of both retirement and non-retirement accounts, financial advice, brokerage services, and mutual fund fees. Investment managers charge wrap fees at **1% to 3%** of the assets they manage.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcc8 Actively Managed vs. Passively Managed Funds\n\nIf the fund is **actively managed** (trying to beat average stock market returns), costs are **higher** than for **passively managed** funds such as index funds, which aim only to mirror the returns of a benchmark index like the S&P 500.\n\n- Fees for actively managed funds can range from **as low as 0.10% to more than 2%** of the fund's assets under management.\n- Passively managed funds, such as index funds and ETFs, aim to mirror the performance of a specific stock index, involve less manual intervention, and **typically have lower fees**.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a1 Performance-Based Fees\nSome funds also use **performance-based fees**, where managers earn additional compensation based on the fund's performance.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Why Fees Matter\nAn expense ratio of **0.75% versus 0.10%** can result in a difference of **thousands of dollars** over 30 years. A fund with high costs must perform **better** than a low-cost fund to generate the same returns for you.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd11 Summary Table\n\n| Fund Type | Typical Fee Range |\n|---|---|\n| Passive / Index Funds | 0.01% \u2013 0.20% |\n| Actively Managed Funds | 0.50% \u2013 2%+ |\n| Wrap Fees | 1% \u2013 3% |\n| 12b-1 (Marketing) Fees | Up to 1% |\n\n> **Tip:** Always check a fund's **prospectus** for the \"Total Annual Operating Expenses\" to understand the full cost before investing.",
